<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8'?>
  <section xml:id="getting-started-first-2d-game-08-adding-spice">
    <title>Adding Spice To the Game</title>
    <introduction>
    <p>Now that you have a working game. Let's consider different ways to
        add additional features.
    </p>
    <p>First, we'll consider add other items for our player to collide with. These can
        be negative items, such as poison that hurts the score, slows down the player, or
        the enemies that defeat the player, or positive items 
        such as treats that improve the score, speed up the character, or give some
        other advantage to the player. There could also be obstacles that just keep the 
        player from going certain places.
    </p>
    <p>
        Each of these additional scenes can have different parts to them. They'll have specific 
        artwork for either their consistent look or their animations. They could have a sound 
        associated with the interaction. They'll have a shape that will determine when objects interact.
        Some artwork is shown in <xref ref="gs-adding-spice-artwork"/> to help stir your imagination
        for ways to spice up your game.
    </p>
        <figure xml:id="gs-adding-spice-artwork">
            <caption>Example Artwork</caption>
            <sidebyside>
               <figure xml:id="art-potion">
                <caption>Potion</caption>
                <image source="art_potion.webp" width="20%">
                    <shortdescription>A potion that could be used as a power-up or power-down</shortdescription>
                </image>
               </figure> 
               <figure xml:id="art-box">
                <caption>Box Obstacle</caption>
                <image source="art_box.webp" width="10%">
                    <shortdescription>A box that could be used as an obstacle</shortdescription>
                </image>
               </figure> 
               <figure xml:id="art-coin">
                <caption>Coin Prize</caption>
                <image source="art_coin.webp" width="10%">
                    <shortdescription>A coin that could be used as a prize</shortdescription>
                </image>
               </figure> 
               <figure xml:id="art-obstacles">
                <caption>Obstacle</caption>
                <image source="art_obstacle.webp" width="20%">
                    <shortdescription>A tree that could be a stationary obstacle</shortdescription>
                </image>
               </figure>
               <figure xml:id="art-enemy">
                <caption>Enemy</caption>
                <image source="art_enemy.webp" width="10%">
                    <shortdescription>A snail Enemy</shortdescription>
                </image>
               </figure>  
            </sidebyside>
        </figure>

    </introduction>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-first-2d-game-08-adding-spice-collision-nodes">
      <title>Collision Nodes</title>
    <introduction>
      <p>As we saw in both the <c>Player</c> scene, and the <c>Mob</c> scene, we started with a 
    2D Node (either an <c>Area2D</c> or a <c>RigidBody2D</c>) and added 2 children,
a <c>Sprite2D</c> (or <c>AnimatedSprite2D</c>) along with a <c>CollisionShape2D</c>.
This basic structure can be used for any number of scenes that construct objects
that can interact through collision detection.</p>
      </introduction>
<subsubsection xml:id="getting-started-first-2d-game-08-adding-spice-collision-nodes-templates">
      <title>Collision Scene Templates</title>
    
        <p>With this knowledge in mind consider making 2 Collision Scene templates.
            A local physics template would have <c>Area2D</c> as the root node, so you, as the 
            developer, will need to implement any physics based changes, while a 
            global physics template would use a <c>RigidBody2</c> as the root node.
            If you wanted, you could make an animated or static version of each for 
            a total of 4 Collision Scene templates to choose from.
        </p>
    <figure>
        <caption>4 Types of Collision Scene Templates</caption> 
        <sidebyside>
      <figure>
        <caption>Local Physics Collision Animated Scene Template</caption>
      <image source="lpcs_animated_template.webp" width="25%">
         <shortdescription>Scene Dock with an Area2D as the root with AnimatedSprite2D and CollisionShape2D as children.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
        <figure>
        <caption>Local Physics Collision Static Scene Template</caption>
      <image source="lpcs_static_template.webp" width="25%">
         <shortdescription>Scene Dock with an Area2D as the root with Sprite2D and CollisionShape2D as children.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>

        <figure>
        <caption>Global Physics Collision Animated Scene Template</caption>
      <image source="gpcs_animated_template.webp" width="25%">
         <shortdescription>Scene Dock with an RigidBody2D as the root with AnimatedSprite2D and CollisionShape2D as children.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
        <figure>
        <caption>Global Physics Collision Static Scene Template</caption>
      <image source="gpcs_static_template.webp" width="25%">
         <shortdescription>Scene Dock with an RigidBody2D as the root with Sprite2D and CollisionShape2D as children.</shortdescription>
     </image>
        </figure>
        </sidebyside>
        
    </figure>
    <p>Using these templates, you can duplicate the scene and rename the
        root Node to match what it's supposed to represent.
    </p>

    </subsubsection>
     
      <subsubsection xml:id="getting-started-first-2d-game-08-adding-spice-collision-shapes">
      <title>Shapes for your CollisionShape2D</title>
    <p>
        Every <c>CollisionShape2D</c> needs to have a shape. There are many shapes to choose from.
        When there are many potential collisions, the simplest shapes such as circles, capsules,
        squares and convex polygons with a low number of vertices are your best choices. Sometimes,
        you may want the shape to fit well with an object that would require a concave polygon.
        It's okay to have a small number of these, but collisions can get expensive to compute,
        and there is higher potential for glitches where an object could get caught inside another 
        object because of the concave nature of each object.
    </p>
    </subsubsection>
      </subsection>
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-first-2d-game-08-adding-spice-artwork">
      <title>Artwork</title>
    <p>
        You have many options when deciding on art that will be part of your game.
        Your two best options are for you to draw the art yourself or to use art that 
        has a simple license such as the creative commons licenses CC0, which is in the 
        public domain and needs no attribution or CC-SA, which requires that you add attribution
        to the source of the artwork. GPL licenses have a requirement that any software that is 
        bundled with the GPL licensed artwork will also have a GPL license, and that limits
        the use of open licenses that allow you to ship programs without including the source code.
        Any items with a copyright need explicit permission from the owner of the copyright in order 
        to use it for an app. The <url href="https://opengameart.org/">open game art</url> website
        has a lot of art you can use to start from, such as the art in <xref ref="gs-adding-spice-artwork" />, but make sure you understand and agree with the license before 
        adding the art to your game.
    </p>
    <p>The open art comes in different forms. Sometimes many images are on
        one sheet, and you'll want to copy them into their own files to be part of
        an animation. You can also make 
        <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/2d/using_tilesets.html#introduction">tilesets</url> 
        and <url href="https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/2d/using_tilemaps.html">tilemaps</url> 
        from these sheets to use them with regular Sprite2D nodes.
        Free tools for image manipulation include 
        <url href="https://gimp.org">GIMP (the GNU Image Manipulation Platform)</url>,
        a graphical program which has features similar to Adobe Photoshop, and 
         <url href="https://imagemagick.org">ImageMagick</url> a command line tool for converting
         images to different formats, for composing images, and much much more. You can also 
         have <em>AI generate art for you</em>, and just like art in the public domain,
         you <url href="https://www.legalshield.com/blog/can-you-copyright-ai-generated-art-your-complete-guide">probably won't have any real claim to it when you use it as a part of your game</url>.
    </p>
</subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="getting-started-first-2d-game-08-adding-spice-sound">
      <title>Sounds for your Collision Templates</title>
      <introduction>
    <p>
        <xref ref="getting-started-first-2d-game-07-finishing-up-sound-effects" text="type-global-title"/>
        shows you how to add global sound to your main scene. You can do this for all of your sounds,
        and it can simplify the process at the expense of having the sound defined separately from
        each node. This trade-off may sound undesirable at first, but if you aren't concerned with 
        localized sound then it allows you to delegate all sound behavior to one spot. The general idea
        in this method is to have each node emit some sort of event signal for an event that could need a sound.
        For example a <c>ball_popped</c> signal could indicate that a ball just popped and has been removed
        from the scene. Since it already was removed from the scene, if the sound were coming from the ball, then
        it would never play, but since the signal was emitted, it can be received by the main scene and then
        the appropriate ball popping sound can play.
    </p>
    <p>Similar to artwork, you do need a source for your sounds. You can make your own by recording
        using a program like <url href="https://www.audacityteam.org">Audacity</url>. You can use 
        music generating tools like <url href="https://lmms.io">LMMS</url>, or you can download
        sounds from distrubution sites like 
        <url href="https://opengameart.org/art-search-advanced?keys=&amp;title=&amp;field_art_tags_tid_op=or&amp;field_art_tags_tid=&amp;name=&amp;field_art_type_tid%5B%5D=12&amp;field_art_type_tid%5B%5D=13&amp;field_art_licenses_tid%5B%5D=4&amp;sort_by=count&amp;sort_order=DESC&amp;items_per_page=24&amp;Collection=">
            OpenGameArt.org (for music and sound efects)</url>.
        When using OpenGameArt.org and browsing the art, clicking the thumbnail will play the sound, and
        clicking the title will bring you to a place to download the sound.
    </p>
    <p> The sound for this chapter is the ogg version of <url href="https://opengameart.org/content/pop">pop, found here</url>.
        We'll be using the scenes from <xref ref="getting-started-step-by-step-instancing-creating-instances" text="type-global-title"/>.
        If you already made the scene with balls instantiated in the main scene, then you may
        want to duplicate that project as a starting point for each of the next subsubsections. You may also refer to <xref ref="gs-instancing-practice-code-download"/> 
        to download the project again,
        rename it to something about sounds and then work from there.
    </p>
</introduction>
    <subsubsection xml:id="getting-started-first-2d-game-08-adding-spice-sound-global">
        <title>Global Sound from Signals</title>
    <p> Recall in <xref ref="gs-instancing-six-balls-figure" text="type-global-title"/> that you have
        six instances of balls that are already in your main scene.
        The first thing we want to do is remove all of the code from the Main.gd script.
        Then, if you don't have the six Ball.tscn instances in the Main.tscn as shown in
        <xref ref="gs-instancing-six-balls-figure"/>, then you should add 6 instances now
        as shown.
    </p>
    <p>
        For the Ball scene, we want to add an empty node script to it.
        In the script we'll add a signal called <c>ball_popped</c>, and then
        in the Signals dock we'll click on the input_event CollisionObject2D signal
        and attach it to a new method call _on_input_event. (This should come up 
        as the default.) In addition, in the Inspector, search for Input and make sure 
        the <em>Pickable</em> box is selected to On.

        The code added to <c>_on_input_event():</c> should be 
    </p>
        <program language="gdscript">
if (event is InputEventMouseButton and 
    event.button_index == MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT and 
    event.pressed):
    ball_popped.emit()
    queue_free()
        </program>
        <p>
            After that code is added to the ball's <c>_on_input_event()</c> method
            and the ball is set to pickable, re-run the main scene, and you should 
            be able to click each ball and see them soundlessly disappear.
            Now you are ready to add the sound code.
        </p>
    <p> The Main scene's script then needs to register for each of the ball's signals, 
        and write code to play the sound for popping the ball. Before changing any code,
        though, we first need to add an <c>AudioStreamPlayer</c> to the Main scene and call
        it <em>BallPopped</em>, that way in Main.md we'll be able to access it with
        <c>$BallPopped</c>. Make sure that you have <c>pop_.ogg</c> downloaded and somewhere in
        your project (possibly a <em>music</em> directory). After creating the <c>$BallPopped</c> 
        Node, go to the inspector and add the pop sound to the stream as shown in <xref ref="first-game-spice-adding-sound-figure"/>.
    </p>
    <figure xml:id="first-game-spice-adding-sound-figure">
        <caption>Add the pop_.ogg file to the AudioStreamPlayer</caption>
        <image source="pop_sound_effect.webp">
            <shortdescription>The pop_.ogg file dialog is shown.</shortdescription>
        </image>
    </figure>
    <p>

        The <c>_ready()</c>, <c>connect_child()</c>, and <c>clicked()</c> methods should look like so:
    </p>
    
    <program language="gdscript">
func _ready() -> void:
    for child in get_children(): 
        if child.has_signal("ball_popped"): 
            connect_child(child) 

func connect_child(child: Node) -> void:
    child.ball_popped.connect(clicked)

func clicked():
    $BallPopped.play()
    </program>
    <p>
        When you run the main scene now, you should be able to click the balls one at a time and 
        each one will make a sound after disappearing.
    </p>

<activity xml:id="first2d-game-spice-sound-three-signal" label="first2d-game-spice-sound-three-signal">
            <title>Sounds on Signal</title>
            <statement><p>This is an example of sounds happening when a 
                signal is received. The <c>Node</c> that is here has nodes as 
                children that emit a <c>ball_popped</c> signal when they are clicked
                before they disappear. The Node also has a child node that is 
                an <c>AudioStreamPlayer</c> named <c>$BallPopped</c>. So each 
                <c>ball_popped</c> from each of the children needs to be connected
                to a function that we'll call <c>clicked</c>, which will play the
                <c>$BallPopped</c> Audio Stream Player. 
                The changing the script below is equivalent to changing the Main script
                in the description above, and the above script should correctly 
                activate the sound when the balls on the left are clicked.
                Without those changes, the balls on the right will make noise when
                clicked, but the balls on the left will remain quiet.
            </p></statement>
            <program xml:id="gs-first2d-signal-sound" interactive='activecode' language="gdscript" label="gs-first2d-signal-sound"
                     pck="gdpractice/L5.P3.click_signals"
                     scene="res://practices/L5.P3.click_signals/practice.tscn">

<code>
extends Node

func _ready() -> void:
    pass

func connect_child(child:Node):
    pass
    
func clicked():
    pass


</code>
<tests>
extends "res://addons/gdpractice/tester/test.gd"


func _init() -> void: #
    side_by_side = true #
    pass

func _build_checks() -> void:
    var ran_scene := _add_simple_check(
        tr("Ran the scene"),
        func() -> String:
            return ""
    )

</tests>
</program>
</activity>
</subsubsection>
<subsubsection xml:id="geting-started-first-2d-game-08-adding-spice-collisions">
    <title>Collisions</title>
    <p>
        Collisions are a great time to add sound. <c>Area2D</c> has the <c>area_entered</c> signal,
        and <c>RigidBody2D</c> has the <c>body_entered</c> signal. <c>CharacterBody2D</c> has to 
        process collisions in the physics process, so there is no signal emitted on collision, but
        that will be covered in a later chapter. Since the balls from the instancing demo are 
        <c>RigidBody2D</c> we'll work with the <c>body_entered</c> signal.
    </p>
    <p> It's probably easiest to start with another copy of the code from 
         <xref ref="gs-instancing-practice-code-download"/>, however if instead you make a copy of the project
         from <xref ref="getting-started-first-2d-game-08-adding-spice-sound-global" />, 
         then you'll need to change the Input of the CollisionObject2D to have Pickable off.
         Make sure that there are ball instances in your Main scene, and then add a script to the
         Ball scene or edit the Ball scene's script.
    </p>
    <p> 
        With the Ball selected in the scene, add an <c>AudioStreamPlayer2D</c> to the Ball
        and rename it <c>SoundEffectPlayer</c>. Add the pop sound to the SoundEffectPlayer,
        then go to the Signal dock and
        find the <c>body_entered</c> signal under <c>RigidBody2D</c>
        connect it to <c>_on_body_entered</c> and then add the following code to 
        <c>_on_body_entered</c>
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
        func _on_body_entered(_body: Node) -> void:
            play_sound()
            ball_popped.emit(self)
    </program>
    <p>
        There are two parts that are missing. <c>play_sound</c>, which we are about
        to write, and the <c>ball_popped</c> signal, which we'll add after.
        First the <c>play_sound</c> method:
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
        func play_sound() -> void:
            $SoundEffectPlayer.play()
    </program>
    <p>
        Next we put the signal near the top of the file after we extend <c>RigidBody2D</c>.
    </p>
    <program language="gdscript">
        signal ball_popped(source)
    </program>
    <p>
        With these 3 parts of code and the <c>body_entered</c> signal connected, 
        this will make it so that every time the ball is hit, it will make a popped sound.
        If you don't want the sound to restart you could add a check
        <c>if not $SoundEffectPlayer.playing:</c> before calling <c>play()</c>.
        The <c>ball_popped</c> signal isn't strictly necessary to make the sound, but
        if you ever want to remove the ball when a collision happens without losing the 
        sound, then you'll need another node to receive the signal and wait for the sound
        to stop playing before calling <c>queue_free()</c> on the Ball. This is shown in the 
        next section using a click instead of a collision.
    </p>
    <activity xml:id="first2d-game-spice-sound-two-collision" label="first2d-game-spice-sound-two-collision">
            <title>Sounds on Collision</title>
            <statement><p>This is just an example of sounds happening when RigidBody2D's collide.
                If you want you can add code to make the ball disappear when the 
                <c>ball_popped</c> signal is emitted like in <xref ref="first2d-game-spice-sound-one"/>. 
                The difference is that the <c>AudioStreamPlayer2D</c> is called <c>SoundEffectPlayer</c>.
                And the sound will already be playing when the signal is emitted, so
                you can just check to make sure the sound has finished playing before freeing the
                ball node.
            </p></statement>
            <program xml:id="gs-first2d-area-sound" interactive='activecode' language="gdscript" label="gs-first2d-area-sound"
                     pck="gdpractice/L5.P2.area_collision_sound"
                     scene="res://practices/L5.P2.area_collision_sound/practice.tscn">

<code>
extends Node

func _ready() -> void:
    pass

func connect_child(child:Node):
    pass
    
func clicked(ball):
    pass


</code>
<tests>
extends "res://addons/gdpractice/tester/test.gd"


func _init() -> void: #
    #side_by_side = true #
    pass

func _build_checks() -> void:
    var ran_scene := _add_simple_check(
        tr("Ran the scene"),
        func() -> String:
            return ""
    )

</tests>
</program>
</activity>
</subsubsection>
<subsubsection xml:id="getting-started-first-2d-game-08-adding-spice-sound-local">
        <title>Local Sound from Signals</title>
<p>
    In this last section we'll use the local signal as a way to pass
    a whole object that when received, a sound can be played and then
    the object will be removed. We can again start with another copy of the code from 
    <xref ref="gs-instancing-practice-code-download"/>.
    When selecting the Ball scene, change the Ball to Area2D, and make sure
    the script says <c>extends Area2D</c> at the top. Make sure the ball is pickable.
    By making it an Area2D, the ball will stay floating. 
    With the Ball selected in the scene, add an <c>AudioStreamPlayer2D</c> to the Ball. 
    Add the pop sound to the AudioStreamPlayer2D.
    Make sure you have a number of
    Ball instances in your main scene.
</p>
<p>
    Once you have Ball instances in the Main scene, go back to the Ball scene
    and connect the <c>input_event</c> signal under <c>CollisionObject2D</c>
    and then add the following code in the Ball.gd script file.
</p>
<program language="gdscript">
    func _on_input_event(viewport: Node, event: InputEvent, shape_idx: int) -> void:
        if event is InputEventMouseButton and event.button_index == MOUSE_BUTTON_LEFT and event.pressed:
            ball_popped.emit(self)
</program>
<p>
    Now you are ready to add code to the main script. First you need to
    connect each <c>ball_signal</c> of each Ball in the main scene to a method
    in the main scene that can process the click. We'll soon call this method
    <c>clicked</c>. 
</p>
<program language="gdscript">
    func _ready() -> void:
        for child in get_children(): 
            if child.has_signal("ball_popped"): 
                connect_child(child) 
</program>
<p>
    Note: the connect_child method doesn't exist yet. Here it is:
</p>
<program language="gdscript">
    func connect_child(child:Node):
        child.ball_popped.connect(clicked)
</program>
<p>
    Note: the clicked method doesn't exist yet. Here it is:
</p>
<program language="gdscript">
    func clicked(ball):
        var sound_player: AudioStreamPlayer2D = ball.get_node("AudioStreamPlayer2D")
        sound_player.play() 
        await sound_player.finished 
        ball.queue_free() 
</program>
<p>
    With these three functions, each ball is connected to the clicked method
    on Main using the ball_popped signal. Since ball_popped is emitted
    when a ball is clicked, the sound will play, it will wait until the sound
    finishes playing, and then the ball will be removed from the scene.
</p>
    <activity xml:id="first2d-game-spice-sound-one" label="first2d-game-spice-sound-one">
            <title>Sounds on Exit</title>
            <statement><p>When making a sound on exit, things are a little more complicated.
                Typically you want the sound to be played by a parent or some node that is not
                about to disappear. In addition standard method calls won't work as expected, so 
                you need to use <term>signals</term>. In this activity, there are a bunch of 
                balls that are on the practice scene. Each of them has a signal called <c>ball_popped</c>
                So, first, when the node is <c>_ready()</c> all of the child nodes with ball 
                popped need to be connected to the
                <c>clicked</c> method of the node. The <c>connect_child</c> method is used 
                as a helper method to simply do the connection.
                The <c>clicked</c> method then needs to get the <c>AudioStreamPlayer2D</c> from
                the ball node passed in, play the stream, wait for it to finish, and then
                call <c>queue_free()</c> on the ball. This causes the ball to make a popping sound
                when it disappears after being clicked.
            </p></statement>
            <program xml:id="gs-first2d-popped" interactive='activecode' language="gdscript" label="gs-first2d-popped"
                     pck="gdpractice/L5.P1.click_reactions"
                     scene="res://practices/L5.P1.click_reactions/practice.tscn">

<code>
extends Node

func _ready() -> void:
    pass

func connect_child(child:Node):
    pass
    
func clicked(ball):
    pass


</code>
<tests>
extends "res://addons/gdpractice/tester/test.gd"


func _init() -> void: #
    #side_by_side = true #
    pass

func _build_checks() -> void:
    var ran_scene := _add_simple_check(
        tr("Ran the scene"),
        func() -> String:
            return ""
    )

</tests>
</program>
</activity>
</subsubsection>
        
</subsection>   
    <subsection xml:id="getting-started-first-2d-game-08-adding-spice-groups">
      <title>Groups: Tagging Scenes for Decisions</title>
      <p>When you are colliding with different scenes that all inherit the
        same class such as <c>Area2D</c> or <c>RigidBody2D</c> it is nice to 
        have an easy way to determine the purpose of the scene. For instance, 
        if you collide with an area that is an enemy, you probably want something
        different to happen than when colliding with a power up.
      </p>
      <p> 
        Godot handles this with <term>groups</term>. A group is a label of a node
        that you can check for during a collision. For instance, the below
        code checks for 3 groups and emits 3 different tags when emitting the <c>collect</c> 
        signal.
      </p>
      <program language="gdscript">
        func _on_body_entered(_body: Node) -> void:
            if _body.is_in_group("prize"):
                _body.collect()
                collect.emit("prize")
            if _body.is_in_group("food")
                _body.collect()
                collect.emit("food")
            if _body.is_in_group("poison")
                _budy.collect()
                collect.emit("poison")
    </program>
      </subsection>
      <subsection xml:id="first-2d-game-spice-summary">
        <title>Summary</title>
      <p>
        This section is about adding spice. The building blocks described here can be used
        to make a variety of different objects that either move or are stationary. They can 
        be shown with a single image, use animations, or the images could changed based on a 
        timer or based on collisions. Sound can be added in various ways either globally or locally.
        Think about the best way to spice up your version of the game, and then 
        try it.
      </p>
      </subsection>
    </section>
